      "text": "We have the equation $|n + 6| = 2 - n$. To solve this, we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression inside the absolute value can be either non-negative or negative, depending on the value of $n$.\n\n1. **Case 1: $n + 6 \\geq 0$**\n\n   In this case, $|n + 6| = n + 6$. Thus, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 2 - n\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for $n$:\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   n + 6 &= 2 - n \\\\\n   n + n &= 2 - 6 \\\\\n   2n &= -4 \\\\\n   n &= -2\n   \\end{aligned}\n\n   We need to verify if this solution satisfies the condition $n + 6 \\geq 0$. Substituting $n = -2$:\n   \\[\n   -2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   Since the condition $n + 6 \\geq 0$ is satisfied, $n = -2$ is a valid solution for this case.\n\n2. **Case 2: $n + 6 < 0$**\n\n   In this case, $|n + 6| = -(n + 6)$. Thus, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   -(n + 6) = 2 - n\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for $n$:\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   -(n + 6) &= 2 - n \\\\\n   -n - 6 &= 2 - n \\\\\n   -6 &= 2 \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n\n   This equation has no solution because $-6 \\neq 2$.\n\n   Hence, there are no solutions for this case.\n\nSummarizing the solutions from both cases, the only valid solution for the equation $|n + 6| = 2 - n$ is $n = -2$. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{-2}$.",
