      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value \\( |x| \\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\n|x| = \n\\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\geq 0 \\\\\n-x & \\text{if } x < 0 \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\( x = n + 6 \\). Therefore, we need to consider the two cases for the absolute value:\n\n1. \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\n2. \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\n\nStep 1: Add \\( n \\) to both sides to eliminate the \\(-n\\):\n\n\\[\nn + 6 + n = 2 - n + n\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Subtract 6 from both sides to isolate \\( 2n \\):\n\n\\[\n2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Divide both sides by 2 to solve for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-4}{2} = -2\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\n\nStep 1: Simplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\nn + 6 = -2 + n\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Subtract \\( n \\) from both sides to eliminate \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn + 6 - n = -2 + n - n\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n6 = -2\n\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution in this case.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe only valid solution is from Case 1, where \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
