      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We need to solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\). The absolute value function \\( |x| \\) can be broken down into two cases: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( n + 6 < 0 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\), so the equation becomes:\n\\[ n + 6 = 2 - n \\]\nAdding \\( n \\) to both sides, we get:\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\nSubtracting 6 from both sides, we get:\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\nDividing by 2, we get:\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\n\nWe need to check if \\( n = -2 \\) satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[ -2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0 \\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n**Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6 \\), so the equation becomes:\n\\[ -n - 6 = 2 - n \\]\nAdding \\( n \\) to both sides, we get:\n\\[ -6 = 2 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there are no solutions in this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
